70
00:09:59.120 --> 00:10:04.149
Mark Blackman: And, additionally, separately, they send signatures.

71
00:10:04.340 --> 00:10:07.989
Mark Blackman: And then additionally, they send, sidecar files.

72
00:10:08.420 --> 00:10:14.349
Mark Blackman: This multi-file upload leads to extra…

73
00:10:14.530 --> 00:10:29.889
Mark Blackman: resources required, confusion, and then also having to tie together these separate files to get a complete picture of the network. This also introduces multiple streams from the consensus node.

74
00:10:30.020 --> 00:10:32.570
Mark Blackman: And, really…

75
00:10:32.850 --> 00:10:45.440
Mark Blackman: Interjects more processing on the node, because right now, today, the node, is the only component tracking state data.

76
00:10:45.900 --> 00:10:51.320
Mark Blackman: And therefore, state services, such as you see at the bottom there, save states.

77
00:10:51.440 --> 00:11:07.200
Mark Blackman: require the consensus node to do this work. And so, we want to really have the consensus nodes focused on consensus and offload as many of these state-based services off of the consensus node to a new node type.

78
00:11:07.760 --> 00:11:18.359
Mark Blackman: Additionally, there's end-to-end delays. Currently, the node will send record files every 2 seconds of wall clock time.

79
00:11:18.500 --> 00:11:22.430
Mark Blackman: And this is, this happens.

80
00:11:22.590 --> 00:11:41.550
Mark Blackman: even if the consensus algorithm, the rounds of consensus may not conform to that two-second timeline. Actually, it does not. So, sometimes you'll have a record file that only has half of a round of consensus data.

81
00:11:42.030 --> 00:11:46.069
Mark Blackman: And, and so we want to, A,

82
00:11:46.570 --> 00:11:56.969
Mark Blackman: get the… get this information off the consensus node as soon as possible, rather than just holding onto it for 2 seconds. And then we also want to align

83
00:11:56.990 --> 00:12:09.380
Mark Blackman: The output of the data closer to the consensus algorithm, where whole rounds of consensus are contained in these output files.

84
00:12:09.850 --> 00:12:18.189
Mark Blackman: So, with those, with those challenges, we have introduced the block string.

85
00:12:18.840 --> 00:12:22.690
Mark Blackman: And there are a few, different ways that

86
00:12:22.800 --> 00:12:40.420
Mark Blackman: this new system, this new output of the network, addresses those issues. First and foremost, as transactions and other network traffic are processed, the consensus nodes will immediately send out

87
00:12:40.470 --> 00:12:59.120
Mark Blackman: those details. So, we no longer have the two-second buffering and waiting to construct the full block. Pieces of the block, or individual components of the block, what we call block items, are sent in real time, and then the block node reconstructs those

88
00:12:59.330 --> 00:13:02.860
Mark Blackman: those block items into blocks. So…

89
00:13:03.160 --> 00:13:10.059
Mark Blackman: This really, reduces that end-to-end delay, and we're seeing this in the data.

90
00:13:10.270 --> 00:13:13.429
Mark Blackman: Substantial cost… or time savings.

91
00:13:13.800 --> 00:13:28.700
Mark Blackman: Additionally, we have consolidated all of the streams into one stream. So, whereas before we had record streams, we had sidecar files, we had all the signatures, and then additionally, we had some

92
00:13:28.970 --> 00:13:34.420
Mark Blackman: Other streams, such as the event streams, which would go in the backup.

93
00:13:34.600 --> 00:13:48.859
Mark Blackman: for audit purposes of the network, and then save states, which are used for disaster recovery. All of that information, including the state data, is now consolidated into this unified output stream.

94
00:13:48.990 --> 00:13:57.990
Mark Blackman: So, very, not only we're adding, more data and…

95
00:13:58.100 --> 00:14:04.959
Mark Blackman: more details on what the network is doing. It is consolidated to one single stream.

96
00:14:06.000 --> 00:14:19.349
Mark Blackman: And then, because we were consolidating, we went through all the data and noticed there was some duplication across the multiple streams in the prior system. So we went ahead and,

97
00:14:19.350 --> 00:14:26.620
Mark Blackman: address that duplication. So, the data is much more efficient, and,

98
00:14:26.680 --> 00:14:31.209
Mark Blackman: We removed all that duplication of data.

99
00:14:32.090 --> 00:14:42.540
Mark Blackman: And then lastly, as I alluded to, we're adding more data. So, now the block node and other downstream, components

100
00:14:42.800 --> 00:15:02.590
Mark Blackman: can receive this block stream and can maintain a database, a state database, just as the consensus nodes do. So, not only from an end-user perspective, having that state data closer to your applications and logic.

101
00:15:03.100 --> 00:15:21.790
Mark Blackman: Additionally, it allows us to offload many of those services the consensus node was responsible for to the block nodes, so that the consensus nodes are, again, solely focused on consensus, and that allows us to have greater scalability over time.

102
00:15:26.690 --> 00:15:30.080
Mark Blackman: So this system is really comprised of

103
00:15:30.210 --> 00:15:36.249
Mark Blackman: three innovations, 3 hips. First of all, we have TSS.

104
00:15:36.420 --> 00:15:38.920
Mark Blackman: This is the new encryption.

105
00:15:39.290 --> 00:15:56.639
Mark Blackman: stack that we… I'm sorry, cryptography stack we are using. And essentially, what this does is, rather than having those individual RSA signatures spent by each of the nodes, and downstream.

106
00:15:57.330 --> 00:16:12.470
Mark Blackman: Devices, such as the mirror node, have to really analyze, gather enough signatures to where you have a threshold to determine if a majority of the network has signed that same record file.

107
00:16:12.560 --> 00:16:24.079
Mark Blackman: So this, not only requires that you get a certain number of records from each of the notes to verify it's the same record being sent

108
00:16:24.210 --> 00:16:38.529
Mark Blackman: by a majority of the nodes on the network. You also have to go through one by one, or the mirror node has to go through one by one, and validate those signatures, from, against those record files.

109
00:16:39.040 --> 00:16:42.909
Mark Blackman: TSS allows us to have a network-wide signature.

110
00:16:43.190 --> 00:16:48.879
Mark Blackman: So there is, one signature, the same signature on every block.

111
00:16:49.020 --> 00:16:57.530
Mark Blackman: is from the network, so you no longer have to get multiple signatures and do the analysis. One proof.

112
00:16:57.670 --> 00:17:02.780
Mark Blackman: And one verification off of any block, from any consensus node.

113
00:17:02.910 --> 00:17:15.780
Mark Blackman: will give you the assurance that a majority of the network has signed off on that block. So that's TSS, we'll go into that in a little more detail. Block Streams is the new data format.

114
00:17:15.819 --> 00:17:35.089
Mark Blackman: So this is, again, taking all of those multiple streams, combining it into one stream, and then additionally allowing extra data insights, such as the inclusion of state data, event data, and other data that could be useful.

115
00:17:35.680 --> 00:17:46.549
Mark Blackman: And then lastly, to tie all this together, we have the block node. The block node, is what mirror nodes will connect to to get this new output data.

116
00:17:46.940 --> 00:17:54.239
Mark Blackman: And, what it does is, A, it receives block items and constructs blocks.

117
00:17:54.510 --> 00:18:05.669
Mark Blackman: It verifies those blocks against the block proof, or the TSS signature, and then it maintains a database, of the state of the network.

118
00:18:05.760 --> 00:18:09.059
Mark Blackman: And then from there, surfaces.

119
00:18:09.090 --> 00:18:25.820
Mark Blackman: a lot of data and state services for downstream customers, but also consensus nodes. For example, if a consensus node is out of sync with the network, it will connect to the block node to resync, or reconnect.

120
00:18:28.620 --> 00:18:31.880
Mark Blackman: So let's get into the block proofs, the TSS.

121
00:18:32.000 --> 00:18:37.239
Mark Blackman: As I mentioned earlier, Every node sends both a record file.

122
00:18:37.360 --> 00:18:42.430
Mark Blackman: And then the signature, that it has produced against that record file.

123
00:18:42.550 --> 00:18:50.579
Mark Blackman: And this requires mirror nodes to download and fetch multiple sets, multiple copies of these files.

124
00:18:50.820 --> 00:18:56.100
Mark Blackman: And then analyze each of the signatures until the threshold is met.

125
00:18:56.340 --> 00:19:06.350
Mark Blackman: With block streams, it's one block proof. From any block, you verify that, you understand that the whole network has or has not signed off on that block.

126
00:19:06.550 --> 00:19:11.630
Mark Blackman: So, a huge, huge upgrade to the cryptography.

127
00:19:11.970 --> 00:19:15.630
Mark Blackman: And we'll get into some more aspects of this.

128
00:19:15.780 --> 00:19:18.999
Mark Blackman: So this is aggregated threshold proofs.

129
00:19:19.270 --> 00:19:26.300
Mark Blackman: They're used to verify blocks, they are… self,

130
00:19:26.480 --> 00:19:31.320
Mark Blackman: self-verifiable. So, if you have one block and one block proof.

131
00:19:31.440 --> 00:19:45.830
Mark Blackman: Whether it's an offline service, or you… you bury it in your yard and, you know, dig it up 10 years from now, that proof can still be verified,

132
00:19:46.060 --> 00:20:02.950
Mark Blackman: through any small library built to do that verification. So, the block and the block proof are self-contained and verifiable without having to query a mirror node, a block node, or a consensus node.

133
00:20:04.170 --> 00:20:09.089
Mark Blackman: That's, very exciting, a very nice upgrade.

134
00:20:09.280 --> 00:20:23.260
Mark Blackman: While it is just a block proof, there's actually 3 components to this. One is standard BLS. BLS is used in other networks, such as Ethereum.

135
00:20:23.330 --> 00:20:40.679
Mark Blackman: And what it allows for is that signature aggregation. So multiple nodes will partially sign the signature, or the BLS, and then once a threshold is, is achieved of those.

136
00:20:40.860 --> 00:20:44.929
Mark Blackman: The sub-signatures, then the proof,

137
00:20:45.280 --> 00:20:51.270
Mark Blackman: Is generated, and you could verify a threshold against that proof.

138
00:20:51.490 --> 00:21:02.360
Mark Blackman: Now, the major issue we have with BLS is all of those sub-signatures, or partial key signatures, are equal weight.

139
00:21:02.710 --> 00:21:09.660
Mark Blackman: So every node on the network has an equal share of influence on a BLS signature.

140
00:21:09.800 --> 00:21:14.670
Mark Blackman: However, in Hedera, we have this concept of consensus weight.

141
00:21:14.820 --> 00:21:20.710
Mark Blackman: So, the network and the, the protocol.

142
00:21:20.750 --> 00:21:39.270
Mark Blackman: is such that a node may have more weight, or more say, into the consensus algorithm. And this consensus weight is calculated from the staking delegations. The amount of staking delegation to a node increases its consensus weight.

143
00:21:40.040 --> 00:21:47.879
Mark Blackman: But for this conversation, you just need to understand each node will have a different weight. So therefore.

144
00:21:48.240 --> 00:21:58.459
Mark Blackman: to allow this BLS signature to support partial weights, we've introduced this algorithm called HINTS.

145
00:21:59.020 --> 00:22:03.560
Mark Blackman: And, basically, this allows us to weight each

146
00:22:03.680 --> 00:22:11.249
Mark Blackman: Signature from the node, all the way to the 64-bit value, consensus weight values.

147
00:22:11.650 --> 00:22:22.330
Mark Blackman: So, HINS is on top of BLS, works as a secondary cryptography service on top of BLS to give us

148
00:22:22.440 --> 00:22:24.509
Mark Blackman: These weighted shares.

149
00:22:25.690 --> 00:22:30.240
Mark Blackman: And then lastly, to verify any,

150
00:22:31.060 --> 00:22:40.260
Mark Blackman: any signature on a network, you need to trust the actual breakdown of the network. Who are the nodes?

151
00:22:40.350 --> 00:22:54.260
Mark Blackman: What are their keys? What is their consensus weight? So you have to be able to prove the address book along with proving the BLS, and hence proof.

152
00:22:54.440 --> 00:22:58.650
Mark Blackman: So to do this, we have a recursive SNARC proof.

153
00:22:58.810 --> 00:23:02.860
Mark Blackman: We call it RAPS, Weighted Rouster Authorization Proofs.

154
00:23:03.140 --> 00:23:12.720
Mark Blackman: But basically, this allows us to prove without a doubt that the current address book is a descendant from the first address book.

155
00:23:12.970 --> 00:23:25.560
Mark Blackman: And what's nice is it's recursive, which means it fully compresses down to a very lightweight proof that is inexpensive to verify.

156
00:23:26.450 --> 00:23:34.420
Mark Blackman: And it also will not grow as the network grows, so it's a set value as far as the size.

157
00:23:35.140 --> 00:23:40.599
Mark Blackman: So, looking at this in action, how this all… Kinda works together.

158
00:23:41.100 --> 00:23:43.930
Mark Blackman: Whenever there is an address book change.

159
00:23:44.480 --> 00:23:47.620
Mark Blackman: a new RAPS proof is generated.

160
00:23:47.770 --> 00:23:54.909
Mark Blackman: And again, this is a recursive proof that proves the current address book is a descendant of the Genesis address book.

161
00:23:55.620 --> 00:24:10.600
Mark Blackman: And, with this, you could determine that all the… or the majority of the nodes on the network have signed off that the nodes, the weights, and the signing keys in the address book are indeed valid.

162
00:24:11.550 --> 00:24:16.190
Mark Blackman: So, whenever the address book changes, a new RAPS proof is produced.

163
00:24:16.830 --> 00:24:34.729
Mark Blackman: Generally, this is a 24-hour cycle. That is because in Hedera, the staking period is 24 hours, and therefore those consensus weights may change, as staking delegations move between and across nodes.

164
00:24:35.020 --> 00:24:41.399
Mark Blackman: So, first thing we have to verify is the RAP's proof, and that is to verify the address book.

165
00:24:41.900 --> 00:24:45.060
Mark Blackman: Once we have that, an individual block.

166
00:24:45.250 --> 00:24:51.789
Mark Blackman: is both, a BLS hints, Proof is created.

167
00:24:52.050 --> 00:25:00.270
Mark Blackman: So, we have a block, each node, with their associated weight, will sign, do partial signatures.

168
00:25:00.670 --> 00:25:06.920
Mark Blackman: And once the threshold, in our case, 50% plus 1,

169
00:25:07.140 --> 00:25:15.140
Mark Blackman: Once that threshold is met, then the block proof is valid and published and ready for verification.

170
00:25:15.380 --> 00:25:34.979
Mark Blackman: So all the nodes will partially sign the block and say, yep, that's the same block I produced, yep, that's the same block I produced. And based on the consensus weight of each node signing it, a threshold is calculated. And once that threshold is passed, then the block proof, or this network-wide

171
00:25:35.050 --> 00:25:37.560
Mark Blackman: Signature is produced.

172
00:25:37.700 --> 00:25:39.559
Mark Blackman: And so,

173
00:25:40.100 --> 00:25:48.760
Mark Blackman: The verification process is to verify the HINTS proof, or the wraps proof, and then the hints and BLS verification keys.

