190
00:27:35.400 --> 00:27:41.680
Mark Blackman: This could be helpful for audits, or, you know, legal analysis.

191
00:27:42.070 --> 00:27:45.819
Mark Blackman: But then again, we're, introducing

192
00:27:46.140 --> 00:27:54.200
Mark Blackman: State data, which will allow you to have state proofs of transactions within a block, but then also maintain

193
00:27:54.340 --> 00:28:02.720
Mark Blackman: A database on a node type, such as a block node, and be able to use that data in real time.

194
00:28:03.790 --> 00:28:23.690
Mark Blackman: This consolidation and deduplication, and then also we've optimized how the mirror node receives this data, which means a lot of cost optimization across participants of the network, less end-to-end latency, the consensus nodes not holding onto the data for up to 2 seconds before it sends it.

195
00:28:24.170 --> 00:28:38.930
Mark Blackman: And then, because of this, this… this streaming of data, even before the block is completed, allows downstream components to preprocess the data, again, leading to lower end-to-end latency.

