00:28:41.060 --> 00:28:42.230
Mark Blackman: Oops, sorry.

197
00:28:43.300 --> 00:28:53.929
Mark Blackman: So, as I mentioned, a block is made up of multiple block items, and we have different types of these blocks, item types.

198
00:28:54.480 --> 00:29:00.440
Mark Blackman: So, first of all, a block header. This is the first block item that says, hey, this is a new block.

199
00:29:00.620 --> 00:29:16.489
Mark Blackman: You have the round header, the event header, event transaction, transaction results. So basically, all of the activity on the network, whether it's for consensus, whether it's for processing transactions.

200
00:29:16.690 --> 00:29:20.520
Mark Blackman: Smart contract execution, or state changes.

201
00:29:21.610 --> 00:29:32.100
Mark Blackman: Each one of these has its own individual type of block item, and again, these items are sent in near real time as they are, processed.

202
00:29:33.650 --> 00:29:35.940
Mark Blackman: And so, for each transaction.

203
00:29:36.060 --> 00:29:45.129
Mark Blackman: you'll see multiple block items make up that transaction. So we'll take a transaction. If you think of a transaction, it's not only

204
00:29:45.720 --> 00:29:48.199
Mark Blackman: the actual transaction

205
00:29:48.380 --> 00:29:56.180
Mark Blackman: That is sent, but you have to look at the result of that transaction, the output back to the user.

206
00:29:56.400 --> 00:30:04.339
Mark Blackman: Any state changes that occur because of that transaction, or in the case of smart contracts, in trace data.

207
00:30:04.590 --> 00:30:13.499
Mark Blackman: So, to get a full transaction requires multiple block types, the same for the, events.

208
00:30:13.720 --> 00:30:21.600
Mark Blackman: And then for each round, as I mentioned, the blocks are now at a round boundary, a consensus round boundary.

209
00:30:21.730 --> 00:30:25.180
Mark Blackman: So we have a header to signify,

210
00:30:25.470 --> 00:30:28.130
Mark Blackman: whether it's a new round. So.

211
00:30:29.540 --> 00:30:38.400
Mark Blackman: The blocks are, contain whole rounds, but a block may contain one to many rounds of data. That is a configuration item.

212
00:30:39.210 --> 00:30:45.800
Mark Blackman: And to start, at least for Hedera, we will be doing one round to the block.

213
00:30:47.380 --> 00:31:01.950
Mark Blackman: And so, basically, these block items are sent. You could see, at the top here, it's starting from the right side. So this is from a consensus node standpoint. First sends the block header, then the round header, etc.

214
00:31:02.960 --> 00:31:07.300
Mark Blackman: And then, the block node will reconstruct

215
00:31:07.570 --> 00:31:10.969
Mark Blackman: these block items into a block. Oop.

216
00:31:12.270 --> 00:31:14.440
Mark Blackman: And then verify the block proof.

217
00:31:14.640 --> 00:31:21.309
Mark Blackman: And then it is accepted into that block node as a verified block.

218
00:31:24.570 --> 00:31:35.180
Mark Blackman: And these blocks, just as before, form a blockchain, the previous block hash is inserted into the present block hash.

219
00:31:35.360 --> 00:31:38.490
Mark Blackman: And therefore, there is a…

220
00:31:39.510 --> 00:31:42.819
Mark Blackman: A running hash, you can think of it as the,

221
00:31:43.960 --> 00:31:46.889
Mark Blackman: As the, blocks are produced.

222
00:31:47.340 --> 00:31:50.790
Mark Blackman: They are cryptographically linked together.

223
00:31:52.240 --> 00:31:56.980
Mark Blackman: And we do, as I mentioned, have state services. This

224
00:31:57.160 --> 00:32:01.050
Mark Blackman: what is great about this implementation, I think, is

225
00:32:02.320 --> 00:32:12.950
Mark Blackman: send this state data, and we not only send the delta, so, hey, add 1HBAR to this account,

226
00:32:13.240 --> 00:32:15.909
Mark Blackman: The… we send the resulting value.

227
00:32:16.420 --> 00:32:19.460
Mark Blackman: From that state change, so if…

228
00:32:19.650 --> 00:32:28.070
Mark Blackman: If we add, 5, and they already had 40, we'll have 45 as the resulting balance.

229
00:32:28.270 --> 00:32:34.180
Mark Blackman: As a result of this, you could take a block that contains your transaction.

230
00:32:34.810 --> 00:32:39.280
Mark Blackman: And, that resulting balance that is published in the block

231
00:32:39.460 --> 00:32:44.170
Mark Blackman: And signed by, the network via the block proof.

232
00:32:44.810 --> 00:32:49.310
Mark Blackman: Allows you to have a state proof, that that

233
00:32:49.460 --> 00:32:54.069
Mark Blackman: Resulting state, has been signed by a majority of the network.

234
00:32:54.990 --> 00:33:01.530
Mark Blackman: These blocks, I don't get into it, but these blocks are in a Merkle Tree structure.

235
00:33:02.000 --> 00:33:08.919
Mark Blackman: So it allows you… Individually, look at, one item within the block.

236
00:33:09.110 --> 00:33:15.730
Mark Blackman: and then Merkle path it to the root, and then verify that root against the block proof.

237
00:33:16.040 --> 00:33:23.649
Mark Blackman: So you could… individual, block items within a block are independently verifiable.

238
00:33:23.840 --> 00:33:30.310
Mark Blackman: And because state data is included in the block now, you could have a very low-cost state proof.

239
00:33:30.460 --> 00:33:35.190
Mark Blackman: For transactions and the resulting state changes in that block.

240
00:33:37.780 --> 00:33:41.619
Mark Blackman: And then lastly, we are introducing a new note type.

