243
00:34:00.860 --> 00:34:06.589
Mark Blackman: And, other value-added services that the community wants to build on top of the block node.

244
00:34:07.750 --> 00:34:12.239
Mark Blackman: We have the concept of Tier 1, Tier 2 block nodes.

245
00:34:12.530 --> 00:34:22.070
Mark Blackman: A Tier 1 block node is essentially the block node that directly receives blocks From the consensus note.

246
00:34:22.500 --> 00:34:29.139
Mark Blackman: They need to be available and up and verifying the network.

247
00:34:29.520 --> 00:34:37.470
Mark Blackman: Because if the consensus nodes cannot send off the blocks and get confirmation that they have been verified in store.

248
00:34:38.060 --> 00:34:41.089
Mark Blackman: They will maintain a buffer of those blocks.

249
00:34:41.370 --> 00:34:44.059
Mark Blackman: And eventually, those buffers fill up.

250
00:34:44.460 --> 00:34:48.759
Mark Blackman: And, back pressure is introduced into the network.

251
00:34:48.880 --> 00:34:55.689
Mark Blackman: And back pressure simply means if a consensus node individually has blocks buffering up.

252
00:34:56.010 --> 00:35:02.470
Mark Blackman: And no block nodes are available to receive and ingest and verify.

253
00:35:02.660 --> 00:35:09.280
Mark Blackman: those blocks, the consensus node will stop Accepting new transactions.

254
00:35:09.390 --> 00:35:14.659
Mark Blackman: So, essentially, the network halts, at least at that consensus node level.

255
00:35:15.820 --> 00:35:28.880
Mark Blackman: And, new transactions cannot be introduced until those blocks are cleared out. So the Tier 1 block nodes not only have the responsibility of distributing the output to the community.

256
00:35:29.000 --> 00:35:33.210
Mark Blackman: But they are directly, involved with the health of the network.

257
00:35:33.400 --> 00:35:40.489
Mark Blackman: So that is why we have the specific Tier 1 block nodes. They maintain state also.

258
00:35:40.700 --> 00:35:45.349
Mark Blackman: Which, it could be a more costly, hardware type.

259
00:35:45.930 --> 00:35:56.000
Mark Blackman: And then, also provide reconnect or resync services to the consensus node. So, Tier 1 block nodes, the focus is really

260
00:35:56.210 --> 00:36:10.289
Mark Blackman: This primary ingestion, ensuring consensus nodes continue to process transactions, and taking care of the validation and publishing of the output of the data, or of the network.

261
00:36:10.690 --> 00:36:18.520
Mark Blackman: A Tier 2 block node is a block node, which receives data from a Tier 1 block node.

